# Resolution
Fixed in commit [`f5324608d187cf81c6fcf605e220d3c03125bdc7`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/f5324608d187cf81c6fcf605e220d3c03125bdc7) (`Bound control-plane heartbeat leases`).

The control-plane authority now owns heartbeat time: `NodeHeartbeat` no longer carries caller-supplied `now_ms`, and `SingleAuthorityControlPlane::heartbeat` takes an authority-provided timestamp when issuing leases. Heartbeat lease duration is now an explicit request capped by `MAX_HEARTBEAT_LEASE_MS`, with overlong requests rejected. Primary serving and deterministic primary selection now require an unexpired lease at the supplied authority time, and regression tests cover both overlong lease rejection and expired-lease primary exclusion.

Verification for the fix:
- `cargo test -p storage control_plane -- --nocapture`
- `cargo clippy -p storage --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`
- `git diff --check`

# Summary
Introduced: the new `control_plane` module trusts heartbeat-provided timestamps and durations and does not enforce lease expiry when deciding whether a node can serve as primary.
The commit introduces `NodeHeartbeat` with caller-controlled `now_ms` and `lease_duration_ms`. `SingleAuthorityControlPlane::heartbeat` computes the lease deadline directly from those values, only rejecting zero duration or arithmetic overflow, then persists and returns that deadline. There is no authority-side clock use, configured maximum lease duration, or expiry check in `NodeControlRecord::can_serve_primary` / `deterministic_pg_primary`. As a result, a node that can send heartbeats for a registered node ID can request a very large lease deadline and continue to be considered healthy/current-epoch for primary selection after it should have expired. In a multihost deployment this undermines the lease/fencing invariant and can permit stale primaries or split-brain writes, risking object/metadata corruption and availability loss. The currently checked-out S3 binary does not appear to expose this module over HTTP yet, but the committed control-plane primitive itself is unsafe for its intended authority role.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm `NodeHeartbeat` lets the heartbeat caller provide both timestamp and lease duration.
- [x] Confirm `heartbeat` computes and stores the lease deadline from those caller-controlled values with no authority clock or maximum lease duration.
- [x] Confirm huge non-overflowing durations are accepted.
- [x] Confirm `can_serve_primary` and `deterministic_pg_primary` have no deadline/current-time expiry check.
- [x] Demonstrate dynamically that an already-expired-looking deadline can still produce `serving=true` and primary selection.
## Report
Validated the finding against commit 9f354065d280203c01847c4ba05bc83dcbe66542. Static review of crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs confirms NodeHeartbeat exposes caller-controlled now_ms and lease_duration_ms at lines 237-243; heartbeat computes lease_deadline_ms as heartbeat.now_ms.checked_add(heartbeat.lease_duration_ms) and only rejects zero/overflow at lines 464-470; it persists last_heartbeat_ms and lease_deadline_ms directly at lines 526-528; can_serve_primary checks only membership, Healthy availability, and observed epoch at lines 182-186; deterministic_pg_primary delegates to can_serve_primary with no current-time or deadline parameter at lines 548-555. Dynamic PoC was added as crates/storage/tests/control_plane_lease_poc.rs and run with `cargo test -p storage --test control_plane_lease_poc -- --nocapture --test-threads=1`. Output showed: `lease_deadline_ms=1 serving=true selected_primary=Some(NodeId(42))`, proving a node with a caller-supplied 1ms deadline is still considered serving and selected as primary. The same PoC showed `requested_now_ms=1000 requested_duration_ms=18446744073709550615 accepted_deadline_ms=18446744073709551615`, proving lease duration is capped only by u64 overflow, not by authority policy. LLDB was used non-interactively on the test binary; at control_plane.rs:471 it showed heartbeat.now_ms=0, heartbeat.lease_duration_ms=1, and computed lease_deadline_ms=1 on both heartbeats, with the call stack entering from the PoC. Valgrind was attempted but is not installed in the container (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). No native memory crash is expected because this is an authorization/lease-fencing logic flaw, not memory corruption.

