## Rationale
Adjusted from high to ignore for current-scope security triage. The code-level claim is valid and validated, but the high severity depends on a future multihost control-plane exposure. Repository evidence shows argmin-s3 starts only existing roles, remote routing is static/configuration-based, storage nodes bind private Unix sockets, and grep found no current call path from HTTP or storage RPC into SingleAuthorityControlPlane/NodeHeartbeat. Without an in-scope attacker-reachable surface, the current probability is effectively zero even though the future-design impact would be serious.
## Likelihood
ignore - Direct exploitation is not possible from the current argmin-s3 HTTP server or storage-node Unix RPC because SingleAuthorityControlPlane::heartbeat is not wired into any runtime entrypoint. Exploitation would require future integration or an out-of-repository caller.
## Impact
ignore - The primitive could have high integrity/availability impact if later used as the authoritative multihost lease/fencing mechanism, because stale primary selection can corrupt object metadata or availability. In this checkout, no current runtime path uses it, so there is no demonstrated current product impact.
## Assumptions
- The in-scope deployed product is the argmin-s3 runtime and workspace code as currently wired in this checkout, not hypothetical future consumers of the public storage crate API.
- No out-of-repository deployment wrapper or custom RPC service exposes crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs.
- Repository artifacts are authoritative for exposure; no Kubernetes, Terraform, Docker, or ingress manifests were present in the checkout.
- A caller must be able to invoke SingleAuthorityControlPlane::heartbeat with a registered node_id.
- The new control_plane module must be integrated into a live control-plane RPC or process path.
- The affected node must have Active or Draining membership and report the current cluster epoch.
- For material impact, multihost primary selection must rely on deterministic_pg_primary/can_serve_primary.
## Path
Current: attacker/client -> argmin-s3 HTTP/storage paths -X-> control_plane::heartbeat
Future-only: registered node -> heartbeat(now_ms,duration) -> stored unbounded lease -> primary selection without expiry -> stale primary/split brain
## Path evidence
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:237-243` - NodeHeartbeat exposes caller-controlled now_ms and lease_duration_ms.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:464-470` - heartbeat rejects only zero duration and arithmetic overflow, then computes the deadline from caller-provided fields.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:526-528` - The authority persists the caller-supplied heartbeat time and derived lease deadline.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:182-186` - can_serve_primary checks membership, Healthy availability, and observed epoch but not lease expiry.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:548-555` - deterministic_pg_primary delegates to can_serve_primary without a current-time or lease-deadline check.
- `crates/storage/src/lib.rs:28-31` - control_plane is a public storage module, so the primitive is part of the crate API, but this alone does not make it runtime reachable.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:135-146` - The binary process roles dispatch to storage-node, combined, frontend, or legacy-local paths; none constructs the new control-plane authority.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:213-221` - Storage-node routing is configured statically with active PG routes and primary_node_id NodeId(0), not via deterministic_pg_primary.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:281-307` - Remote frontend builds topology from configured epoch/socket maps and Unix storage-node clients, not from the new control-plane authority.
- `crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:529-546` - The storage-node service binds a Unix domain socket, not a network control-plane heartbeat endpoint.
- `crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:7698-7723` - The storage-node socket directory must be private, limiting storage RPC exposure to local/internal users.
- `plans/multihost-transition-plan.md:7022-7031` - The plan describes the new control-plane authority as the first Phase 11 slice and intentionally single-authority/future distributed work, supporting that this is foundation work rather than fully wired runtime exposure.
## Narrative
The underlying lease-fencing bug is real: NodeHeartbeat includes caller-supplied time and duration, heartbeat stores now_ms + lease_duration_ms with only zero/overflow checks, and primary selection does not check expiry. The validation PoC demonstrates the primitive behavior. The severity should not remain high for the current repository state because this primitive is not reachable from argmin-s3 HTTP routes or the storage-node Unix RPC; current multihost configuration still uses static cluster epochs/routes and primary NodeId(0). Thus this is a serious design defect to fix before control-plane exposure, but it is not an exploitable current security vulnerability in the checked-out product.
## Controls
- HTTP S3 requests are routed then authenticated with SigV4 before dispatch, except OPTIONS CORS preflight.
- Default ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR is 127.0.0.1:9000, although operators can configure another address.
- Storage-node RPC uses Unix domain sockets rather than TCP.
- Storage-node socket paths must be absolute and their parent directory must be private.
- Frontend credentials and SSE wrapping keys are supplied through environment variables.
- TLS is optional via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH.
- No current control-plane heartbeat transport, route, or authorization layer exists for NodeHeartbeat.
## Blindspots
- Static-only review cannot see external/downstream applications that may import the public storage crate and expose control_plane themselves.
- No deployment manifests were present, so exposure is inferred from code defaults and configuration handling rather than live infrastructure.
- The validation PoC exercised the library primitive directly, not through a normal runtime attacker surface.
- Future Phase 11 commits may wire this module into a real control-plane transport, which would require re-rating.
